Tiles for the things Angband does not have
==========================================

    audit-tiles.py              what each set does not draw; fails on a gap
    borrow-tiles.py             give a thing with no tile one that exists
    make-terrain.py             draw the wilderness for sets that have none
    make-services.py            draw the six service buildings, and the
                                Statue Store's door
    build-neon.py               build the Neon set, generated whole
    import-zangband-tiles.py    bring Adam Bolt's Zangband uniques into his
                                Angband sheet, and Zangband's figurine and
                                statues into his and the 8x8 one

Run the audit first.  It is the only one that answers the question that
matters -- is anything in the game still without a tile -- and it exits
non-zero if the answer is yes.  The things left as letters on purpose are
uniques, two traps that are not pictures, and the objects its OBJECT_SKIP
names for the sets with no picture of them: so far, the magical figurine and
the ten statues in the Nomad and Gervais sets.  The other scripts each check what they wrote,
which is a different and smaller claim.

Where the gaps come from
------------------------

ZangbandTK adds 389 monsters, seventeen terrain features, six places to walk
into that are not shops, and a run of objects; Angband's four tilesets have art
for none of it, because Angband has none of it.  In graphics mode every one of
those rendered as an ASCII letter in the middle of a drawn dungeon.

Three mechanisms close that, in increasing cost:

**Borrow** -- `borrow-tiles.py`.  Most of it.  A thing with no tile takes the
picture of a thing that has one: monsters match on `base:`, preferring the same
`color:` and then the nearest `depth:`; objects match on `type:`, then colour,
then nearest `level:`; traps match on the code trap.txt groups them under.  No
art is drawn.  It writes `graf-ztk.prf` into each set and adds one `%:` line so
the set loads it.

**Draw a rule** -- `make-terrain.py` for ground, `make-services.py` for the six
services.  Reached when nothing similar exists to borrow from.  Both generate
into a row appended to the set's own sheet, sampling that set's own palette, so
the new art sits in the set rather than beside it -- and both reclaim their row
on a re-run, so running them twice is the same as running them once.  Being
generated has a licensing dividend as well: the art is this project's own.

**Draw a picture** -- not done for these sets, and not planned.  Four sheets at
three tile sizes is four times the work and four chances to be inconsistent.

Uniques are not borrowed for
----------------------------

The rule is DEC-18, and it is the most important thing here: **a picture may
not assert an identity it does not have.**  A borrowed tile is a fair stand-in
for a common monster and a lie about a named one, because what it borrows is
some *other* named monster's portrait.  An ASCII letter says "not drawn yet".
A wrong portrait says something false.

Stated that way rather than as "no tiles for uniques", because the latter is
not what the project does and never was.  The Neon set gives every unique a
tile -- a base silhouette in the monster's own colour -- and Oberon, Dworkin,
Mandor and the Mouth of Sauron all share `0x9D:0x9C` there, with eleven others.
That is allowed: a violet humanoid asserts violet and humanoid, which is true
of all of them.  1,013 monsters share 31 silhouettes in that set and none of
them is a lie.

The same rule is what lets `import-zangband-tiles.py` give sixty-five of them
Adam Bolt's own drawings, since a drawing of Robin Hood used for Robin Hood
asserts exactly what is true.  After that import, 62 uniques render as text in
Adam Bolt's set and around 125 in the other three, and the audit does not count
any of it as a gap.

Mimics are left out too, and for a harder reason
------------------------------------------------

A feature with `mimic:` in terrain.txt is never drawn as itself, so it cannot
have a tile gap.  `map_info()` replaces its index with the mimic's before the
renderer sees it (cave-map.c:99 and :133), the knowledge screen's feature list
skips mimics outright (ui-knowledge.c:2488), and `angband_feature` applies the
same filter -- three independent paths, no fourth.

Two features are mimics: SECRET mimics GRANITE, and INVIS_WALL, which is
nightmare mode's wall that pretends to be floor, mimics FLOOR.  INVIS_WALL is
this project's own and no shipped set declares it, so until the audit learned
about `mimic:` it read as a gap in all five sets.  Giving it the floor tile
would have worked and would have been worse: five sets of data nothing reads.

Nothing else here makes a claim that strong, which is why BASE_ALIAS exists.
Angband has no fish, no wall monsters and no animated weapons, so those had
nothing to borrow and used to be letters; a fish drawn as a snake asserts only
that it is long and legless, which it is.

Four ways this goes wrong in silence
------------------------------------

Every one of these produced a file that parsed cleanly and did nothing:

* **Case.**  The engine compares names with `my_stricmp`, and the shipped files
  do not agree with the data: the prf files say "Filthy street urchin" where
  monster.txt says "filthy street urchin".  A case-sensitive check reports 629
  missing monsters in a set that draws all of Angband.
* **Object names.**  `lookup_sval()` compares against
  `obj_desc_name_format()` of each kind's name, so `& Small wooden chest~` has
  to be written "Small wooden chest".  The raw form resolves to nothing, and an
  sval that does not exist is not an error.
* **Substring monster names.**  `lookup_monster()` falls back to a substring
  match, so a misspelt name binds to whatever contains it.
* **Coordinates.**  The renderer takes the cell index modulo the sheet size
  rather than complaining, so a coordinate that is not really in the sheet
  draws some other picture.

`--check` is what proves these stayed avoided.  Names come from the data files
and borrowed coordinates are copied from lines the set already uses, so all
four are avoided by construction -- but by construction is a claim that needs
testing, not asserting.

Every script now has one, and three of the four write nothing:

    audit-tiles.py                        read-only
    borrow-tiles.py --check               read-only
    make-services.py --check              read-only
    make-terrain.py --check               read-only
    import-zangband-tiles.py --check      read-only
    build-neon.py --check                 **regenerates first**, then reports.
                                          Harmless by hand, because the set is
                                          generated whole and the output is
                                          deterministic -- but it is why
                                          check-build does not run it

make-terrain.py used to have no --check, and a bare run did the destructive
thing: it rewrote the row and truncated each sheet back to it, dropping the
services row below.  Somebody reaching for it to find out whether anything was
wrong got three rewritten sheets instead, with a paragraph in this file as the
only warning.  It now refuses without an explicit `--write`, and `--write`
names the sets whose services row it just dropped and tells you to re-run
make-services.py.

What runs these
---------------

    scripts/check-build    audit-tiles, make-terrain --check,
                           make-services --check, borrow-tiles --check,
                           import-zangband-tiles --check
    linux.yaml, Tiles job  the same five, plus regenerating the Neon set and
                           diffing graf-neo.prf, which catches generated data
                           that has gone stale rather than missing

No paths filter on the CI job, deliberately.  The way this went red was a
gamedata change invalidating a tiles artifact -- MON_PSI_DRAIN added to
projection.txt, no set regenerated -- so a filter would have to name every
directory that can invalidate a tileset, and that list is a guess which has
already been wrong once.

Replacing a placeholder with real art
-------------------------------------

Delete the line from the generated file and add a proper mapping to the set's
own prf.  Re-running will not put it back: a generated line only ever covers a
thing that had no tile from any other file.

One ordering rule
-----------------

Three scripts append rows to the same sheet, and each truncates back to its own
row when re-run -- so a later one's rows are destroyed by an earlier one.  The
order is:

    make-terrain.py --write        terrain row
    make-services.py --write       services row, below terrain
    import-zangband-tiles.py --write   imported Zangband tiles, below both

Run any of them and every one after it has to be run again.  `make-terrain.py
--write` names the sets whose services row it just dropped; `make-services.py
--check` and `import-zangband-tiles.py --check` both assert that nothing
generated sits below them.
